sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch03-11> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Among two-color substitution systems that allow elements to be destroyed, those with slow growth produce only simple repetitive patterns, but allowing three or four colors immediately makes patterns as random-looking as those of cellular automata possible.";
    dc:type "computational-observation";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Substitution systems with deletion need three or more colors for complexity";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 3, “The World of Simple Programs”; section “Substitution Systems” (from p. 82), p. 86. Scope: neighbor-dependent substitution systems with creation and destruction of elements"
}
